&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;51.7%&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;47.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;53.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;half-gain noise&lt;/strong&gt; (accuracy halfway between base 60.8% and adapter 94.5%) is&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;σ = 3.47 × RMS&lt;/strong&gt;. Above that the behavior does not decay gracefully back to the base; it&lt;br&gt;
falls to chance. The adapter's signal is replaced by noise, not by the original model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What this looks like
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A plateau, then a cliff.&lt;/strong&gt; From 0 to 2× RMS the behavior is essentially unchanged. At
3× it is damaged; at 5× it is gone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The collapse overshoots the base.&lt;/strong&gt; At high noise SST-2 is ~50%, below the base 60.8%.
The perturbation does not restore the base; it destroys the adapter's contribution and
leaves a chance-level classifier.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Quantization is far inside the plateau.&lt;/strong&gt; The 8-bit and 4-bit adapters are exact on this
task; their quantization noise is much smaller than the 3.5× RMS meltdown scale.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;This is a first-order-looking curve&lt;/strong&gt;, but one adapter and one task are not enough to
claim a universality class.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Caveats
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gaussian weight noise is not quantization, pruning, or fine-tuning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One adapter, one task, 400 rows, one seed per σ, logit scoring.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The adapter RMS is global; per-tensor noise scales could change the curve.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No finite-size scaling or susceptibility measurement yet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Next experiments
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Repeat on AG News, Dolly QA and the watermark trigger; does the meltdown scale track
adapter RMS or task margins?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Noise only A, only B, or rank-wise; which part of the LoRA carries the behavior?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Measure the &lt;strong&gt;susceptibility&lt;/strong&gt; (variance of the order parameter across noise seeds) near
the transition.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compare with quantization: q4/q8 are at a much lower effective temperature; how much
headroom is there before a 3-bit adapter melts?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test whether a &lt;strong&gt;behavioral vaccine&lt;/strong&gt; raises the melting temperature.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The point is not that a LoRA is thermodynamic. The point is that "how much noise can this&lt;br&gt;
behavior survive?" is a measurable curve, and the answer is not "linearly less accuracy per&lt;br&gt;
bit."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Coding assistants are good at well-known networks and tend to guess about smaller ones. To give them something accurate to read about Zucoin's Splitchain layer 1 network, we put the &lt;a href="https://myzucoins.org" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MyZucoins&lt;/a&gt; guides and developer docs behind an open Model Context Protocol (MCP) server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MyZucoins provides tools, services and developer resources for Splitchain. This post shows how to connect the MCP server to Claude or Cursor, and how to call it directly. It needs no account, subscription or API key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: this is written by the team behind MyZucoins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the server does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The endpoint is &lt;code&gt;https://myzucoins.org/mcp&lt;/code&gt; (Streamable HTTP, no authentication). It is read-only content: it cannot access wallets, sign or send transfers, read accounts or give live prices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tool&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;search_articles&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Search published help, developer tutorials, news and deep dives&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;get_article&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Read a full article by its slug, as plain text&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;list_topics&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;List topics with article counts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;list_pages&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Find public Zucoin, Splitchain, wallet, safety and network statistics pages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Connect it to an assistant
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Claude Code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;claude mcp add --transport http myzucoins https://myzucoins.org/mcp
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In Cursor, add it to &lt;code&gt;mcp.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"mcpServers"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"myzucoins"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://myzucoins.org/mcp"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then ask something like: "Using MyZucoins, how do I reinstall the Zucoin wallet app without losing my wallet?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Call it directly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MCP over HTTP is JSON-RPC, so you can try it with &lt;code&gt;curl&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -s -X POST https://myzucoins.org/mcp \
  -H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Content-Type: application/json'&lt;/span&gt; \
  -H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream'&lt;/span&gt; \
  -d &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/call",
       "params":{"name":"search_articles",
                 "arguments":{"query":"wallet backup","limit":2}}}'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Trimmed, one result looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"slug"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"how-do-i-reinstall-the-zucoin-wallet-app"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"kind"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"help"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"How do I reinstall the Zucoin wallet app?"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"excerpt"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Back up your current wallet, reinstall the Zucoin wallet app from a preferred source, and restore the wallet from your valid backup file."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"topics"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"troubleshoot"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Troubleshoot"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://myzucoins.org/how-do-i-reinstall-the-zucoin-wallet-app/"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Pass the &lt;code&gt;slug&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;get_article&lt;/code&gt; to read the full guide, with its source link and date.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A few tips for agents
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Call &lt;code&gt;list_topics&lt;/code&gt; to narrow a search, for example to Developer Docs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;limit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;next_offset&lt;/code&gt; to page through results.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cite the article &lt;code&gt;url&lt;/code&gt; in answers, so people can read the original.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Never ask a user for a wallet backup or private key. The guides themselves warn against sharing them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Full documentation is at &lt;a href="https://myzucoins.org/mcp-api-docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;myzucoins.org/mcp-api-docs&lt;/a&gt;. If you build something with it, we'd like to hear what's missing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A hardened AOS-CX baseline puts SSH on the management VRF behind an allow-list, authenticates logins centrally with a local fallback, moves SNMP to v3 only, and authenticates NTP. On the access ports it adds port security, BPDU guard or loop protection, DHCP snooping, and ARP inspection, with unused ports shut down in a blackhole VLAN. As on any platform, static-IP cameras need explicit handling before ARP inspection goes on. Plan for that first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The syntax below was checked against the HPE AOS-CX Command-line interface Reference Guide for 8100 and 8360 Switch Series, published February 2026. Other series such as the 6200 and 6300 share most of it, but confirm against the guide for your model and release. Addresses are from the 192.0.2.0/24 documentation range, and every secret is a placeholder. The Cisco equivalent is in &lt;a href="https://hans.study/hardening-cisco-catalyst-switches-ios-xe-baseline/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;hardening Cisco Catalyst switches&lt;/a&gt;, and the audit side is in the &lt;a href="https://hans.study/standards-guidance/switch-configuration-audit-checklist-for-security-networks/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;switch configuration audit checklist&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Management plane
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SSH is disabled until the admin password is set, and it is enabled per VRF. It will not start without it. Put it on the management VRF, restrict who can connect, and turn off Telnet and the web interface where you do not use them. The CLI reference states that the Telnet server is disabled by default, so the explicit line below is a guard against someone enabling it later.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ssh server vrf mgmt
ssh server allow-list
 ip 192.0.2.0/24
 &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
ssh maximum-auth-attempts 3
no telnet server vrf mgmt
no https-server vrf mgmt
session-timeout 10
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Enabling the allow-list restarts the SSH servers and ends active sessions, and an enabled list with no entries blocks everyone. Apply it from the console or from a session inside the permitted range. Test it. Keep the HTTPS server only if you use the REST API or web interface, and then keep it on the management VRF.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication comes next. Create named users in the built-in groups, point SSH and console logins at TACACS+ with local last, and test the fallback before you leave the room. AOS-CX stores local passwords hashed, so there is no separate secret type to choose.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;user ops1 group administrators password
tacacs-server host 192.0.2.20 key plaintext &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;strong-secret&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; vrf mgmt
aaa authentication login ssh group tacacs &lt;span class="kd"&gt;local&lt;/span&gt;
aaa authentication login console group tacacs &lt;span class="kd"&gt;local&lt;/span&gt;
aaa authentication limit-login-attempts 5 lockout-time 300
banner motd ^
This system is private property of &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;ORGANIZATION&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;. Authorized use only.
Activity is logged and monitored.
^
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The guide warns that command authorization has no fail-through, so a wrong TACACS+ authorization setup can leave a switch unable to run commands. Always end the group list with local, and prove the setup on a lab switch first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Time, SNMP, and logging
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NTP can be tied to a trusted key so the switch only syncs with a source that proves itself, which protects log timestamps from a spoofed time server on the camera VLAN. SNMPv3 users get authentication and privacy, and the switch can be told to drop v1 and v2c entirely. Syslog goes out over the management VRF.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ntp authentication-key 10 sha1 &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;strong-secret&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; trusted
ntp authentication
ntp server 192.0.2.30 key 10
ntp vrf mgmt
ntp &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
snmp-server snmpv3-only
snmpv3 security-level auth-privacy
snmpv3 user monitor auth sha256 auth-pass plaintext &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;strong-secret&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; priv aes priv-pass plaintext &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;strong-secret&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; access-level ro
logging 192.0.2.10 severity info vrf mgmt
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The guide notes that a # character in an NTP key stops synchronization once authentication is on, so choose keys without one. The logging command also supports TCP and TLS transports, and TLS is the better choice when your collector can present a certificate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Control plane basics
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AOS-CX applies a predefined control plane policing policy named default at first startup. Review it with &lt;code&gt;show copp-policy&lt;/code&gt; rather than rewriting it. Leave it alone. Rate limits on ingress ICMP and broadcast traffic on edge ports add a second layer, and the management plane is already limited by the VRF and allow-list above.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Access ports
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Edge ports get an access VLAN, edge port type, BPDU guard, loop protection, a broadcast rate limit, and port security, all applied the same way to every port that faces a camera, reader, or workstation. The client limit defaults to 1. Sticky learning keeps the first MAC the port sees. A violation shuts the port down, and auto-recovery brings it back after a timer.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;spanning-tree bpdu-guard timeout 300
port-access port-security &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
interface 1/1/1
 no routing
 vlan access 100
 spanning-tree port-type admin-edge
 spanning-tree bpdu-guard
 loop-protect
 rate-limit broadcast 1 percent
 port-access port-security &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
 port-access security violation action shutdown
 port-access security violation action shutdown auto-recovery &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
 port-access security violation action shutdown recovery-timer 300
 port-access port-security
  client-limit 1
  sticky-learn &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Port security with one client catches an unmanaged switch added for extra drops, while BPDU guard catches a managed switch or a loop through one. Loop protection is a separate mechanism that sends its own control packets, so it also catches loops that never involve spanning tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;DHCP snooping is enabled globally and per VLAN, and only the ports that face the real DHCP server, usually the uplinks or the LAG toward the distribution layer, are trusted. Everything else stays untrusted. From release 10.14 the command is &lt;code&gt;dhcp-snooping&lt;/code&gt;, and earlier releases use &lt;code&gt;dhcpv4-snooping&lt;/code&gt;. ARP inspection is enabled per VLAN on the 8100 and 8360 from 10.12.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dhcp-snooping
vlan 100
 dhcp-snooping
 arp inspection
interface lag 1
 dhcp-snooping trust
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;ARP inspection discards ARP packets that carry an invalid IP-to-MAC binding, so a camera with a static address and no lease is at risk of being dropped. That is the trap. Convert cameras and door controllers to DHCP reservations where you can. For devices that must stay static, the guide documents &lt;code&gt;ipv4 source-binding&lt;/code&gt;, which adds a static entry to the binding database.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ipv4 source-binding 100 192.0.2.50 00:11:22:33:44:55 1/1/5
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The guide documents how to enable ARP inspection and view its trust state per interface, but I could not find a command to set a port as trusted in it. Check the security guide for your release, and keep uplinks in mind when you test, because a wrongly untrusted uplink drops ARP between switches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Unused ports
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unused ports go into a VLAN with no routed interface, get a description, and are shut down.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;vlan 666
 name BLACKHOLE
interface 1/1/24
 no routing
 vlan access 666
 description UNUSED
 shutdown
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Backups and firmware
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Copy the running configuration to a remote server over SFTP or SCP on a schedule, using the management VRF, and diff each copy against the last. The guide lists the copy forms for running configuration and checkpoints.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;copy running-config sftp://backup@192.0.2.40/sw01.cfg vrf mgmt
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For firmware, read the release notes and the HPE security bulletins for the target version before upgrading. AOS-CX keeps primary and secondary images, so stage the new image on the secondary partition and keep the known-good one until the switch has run clean for a day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checking your work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://hans.study/tools/switch-config-audit/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;switch config audit tool&lt;/a&gt; checks a configuration you paste or upload against a published checklist. The &lt;a href="https://hans.study/switch-config-audit/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;switch config audit&lt;/a&gt; is the remote review across a whole estate, and the &lt;a href="https://hans.study/learning/network-hardening-level-1/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Network Hardening Level 1&lt;/a&gt; course and the &lt;a href="https://hans.study/training/network-and-windows-hardening/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Network and Windows Hardening&lt;/a&gt; training cover the reasoning behind each control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Book a working session at &lt;a href="https://hans.study/book/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;/book/&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  CVE-2026-88984: RBAC Authorization Bypass in Kubeflow Pipelines Multi-User API Server
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vulnerability ID:&lt;/strong&gt; CVE-2026-88984&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CVSS Score:&lt;/strong&gt; 7.2&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Published:&lt;/strong&gt; 2026-11-09&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CVE-2026-88984 (GHSA-ghgh-57qv-h687) is an incorrect authorization vulnerability affecting Kubeflow Pipelines (KFP) prior to version 2.16.1 in multi-user deployments. The multi-user API server treats the empty Kubernetes namespace associated with shared or global pipelines as an unconditional authorization exception across all API verbs. Consequently, any authenticated user can modify, overwrite, or delete shared pipeline definitions and pipeline versions without possessing the required Kubernetes RBAC permissions, compromising the integrity and availability of global machine learning workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An authorization bypass in the Kubeflow Pipelines (&amp;lt; 2.16.1) multi-user API server allows any authenticated user to modify or delete shared (global) pipelines and pipeline versions because the server unconditionally skips Kubernetes RBAC checks when a pipeline's namespace is empty.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Technical Details
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CVE ID&lt;/strong&gt;: CVE-2026-88984 (GHSA-ghgh-57qv-h687)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Vulnerability Class&lt;/strong&gt;: CWE-863: Incorrect Authorization / CWE-285: Improper Authorization&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CVSS v4.0 Score&lt;/strong&gt;: 7.2 High (CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:L/UI:N/VC:N/VI:H/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;EPSS Score&lt;/strong&gt;: 0.31% (24.09th Percentile)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Attack Vector&lt;/strong&gt;: Network (Authenticated Low-Privileged Tenant via KFP Multi-User API)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Security Impact&lt;/strong&gt;: Unauthorized Modification and Deletion of Shared/Global Pipelines&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Affected Versions&lt;/strong&gt;: Kubeflow Pipelines &amp;lt; 2.16.1&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CISA KEV Status&lt;/strong&gt;: Not Listed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Affected Systems
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kubeflow Pipelines (multi-user deployments)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ghcr.io/kubeflow/kfp-api-server&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Kubeflow Pipelines&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;gt;= 2.0.2, &amp;lt; 2.16.1 (Fixed in: &lt;code&gt;2.16.1&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Code Analysis
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Commit: &lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/commit/186dd1f3b0403af841c9b4ea7ab94f405339ef6c" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;186dd1f&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;fix(backend): enforce pipeline authorization (#13288)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;--- a/backend/src/apiserver/server/pipeline_server.go&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+++ b/backend/src/apiserver/server/pipeline_server.go&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gu"&gt;@@ -1262,8 +1262,21 @@ func (s *BasePipelineServer) canAccessPipeline(ctx context.Context, pipelineId s&lt;/span&gt;
            resourceAttributes.Name = pipeline.Name
        }
    }
&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-   // Skip authentication if the namespace is empty to enable shared pipelines in multi-user mode&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+   // Skip authorization for read-only operations on shared pipelines in multi-user mode.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+   // Write operations (create, update, delete) on shared pipelines must still be authorized&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+   // against the KFP system namespace, since shared pipelines have no namespace of their own.&lt;/span&gt;
    if s.resourceManager.IsEmptyNamespace(resourceAttributes.Namespace) {
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       if resourceAttributes.Verb == common.RbacResourceVerbGet || resourceAttributes.Verb == common.RbacResourceVerbList {&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+           return nil&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       }&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       resourceAttributes.Namespace = common.GetPodNamespace()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       resourceAttributes.Group = common.RbacPipelinesGroup&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       resourceAttributes.Version = common.RbacPipelinesVersion&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       resourceAttributes.Resource = common.RbacResourceTypePipelines&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       err := s.resourceManager.IsAuthorized(ctx, resourceAttributes)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       if err != nil {&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+           return util.Wrapf(err, "Failed to access shared pipeline %s. Check if you have permission to %s shared pipelines", pipelineId, resourceAttributes.Verb)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       }&lt;/span&gt;
        return nil
    }
    resourceAttributes.Group = common.RbacPipelinesGroup
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Mitigation Strategies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Upgrade Kubeflow Pipelines to version 2.16.1 or later to enforce Kubernetes SubjectAccessReview checks against the KFP system namespace for mutating operations on shared pipelines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Configure explicit Kubernetes RoleBindings in the KFP installation namespace (e.g., 'kubeflow') for legitimate administrators or CI/CD service accounts that require write access to global pipelines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Deploy Istio AuthorizationPolicy or API gateway rules to restrict mutating HTTP methods (POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH) on pipeline management and multipart upload endpoints (/apis/v1beta1/pipelines/upload*) to trusted administrative identities.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Audit the KFP metadata database and Envoy/API server access logs for unauthorized pipeline version injections or deletions targeting shared pipelines with empty namespaces.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Remediation Steps:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identify the current Kubeflow Pipelines version deployed in the cluster by checking the image tag on the 'ml-pipeline' deployment in the 'kubeflow' namespace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Update the KFP manifests or Kustomize overlays to use release 2.16.1 (specifically updating 'ghcr.io/kubeflow/kfp-api-server:2.16.1', 'ghcr.io/kubeflow/kfp-driver:2.16.1', and 'ghcr.io/kubeflow/kfp-launcher:2.16.1').&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Apply a Kubernetes RoleBinding in the KFP system namespace binding the 'kubeflow-edit' ClusterRole to any specific User or ServiceAccount subjects responsible for publishing or maintaining shared pipelines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verify the fix by attempting to delete or create a version on a shared pipeline using a standard tenant user token and confirming that the API server returns an authorization error referencing the KFP system namespace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/security/advisories/GHSA-ghgh-57qv-h687" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Security Advisory GHSA-ghgh-57qv-h687&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/pull/13288" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Kubeflow Pipelines Pull Request #13288: fix(backend): enforce pipeline authorization&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/commit/186dd1f3b0403af841c9b4ea7ab94f405339ef6c" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Fix Commit 186dd1f3b0403af841c9b4ea7ab94f405339ef6c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/releases/tag/2.16.1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Kubeflow Pipelines Release 2.16.1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-88984" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official CVE.org Record for CVE-2026-88984&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-88984" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NVD Vulnerability Detail for CVE-2026-88984&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://cvereports.com/reports/CVE-2026-88984" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Read the full report for CVE-2026-88984 on our website&lt;/a&gt; for more details including interactive diagrams and full exploit analysis.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short answer: treat &lt;code&gt;Content-Type&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Content-Disposition&lt;/code&gt; as part of the private export contract, persist them with the object, and create the presigned download only after verifying that metadata. A browser opening a PDF or CSV in a tab is usually a response-header problem, not a reason to make the object public.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction matters in a media system. A tenant may export a CSV of media activity or a PDF review packet, while the storage key should reveal neither the tenant's preferred filename nor its internal account number. The key is an isolation boundary; the download filename is presentation. Mixing those jobs creates confusing bugs and occasionally leaks information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What should a private object storage export guarantee?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would record four invariants in the architecture decision record:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Invariant&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Implementation responsibility&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Failure it prevents&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The object is private&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Authorization service issues a short-lived presigned link&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A copied URL becomes an uncontrolled public file address&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The bytes have a declared type&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exporter selects &lt;code&gt;application/pdf&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;text/csv&lt;/code&gt; from the generated format&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A client guesses from an internal key or a generic binary type&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The response asks for a download&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stored metadata includes &lt;code&gt;Content-Disposition: attachment&lt;/code&gt; and a safe filename&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The browser opens a PDF or CSV inline&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tenant access is checked before signing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The signing service resolves tenant and object ownership from a server-side record&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A valid signature is mistaken for a valid business authorization&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The last invariant is easy to omit because a presigned URL feels like the authorization step. It is only the delivery credential. It should be minted after the application has established that the requesting user can access that export, and it should carry an expiry appropriate to the handoff. A link sent to a media client is not a replacement for an export record with an owner, format, and status.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The browser is the final observer of the system, so the test needs to reach it. An HTTP client that receives &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; and the expected bytes has proved transport. It has not proved the saved filename or whether a PDF opens inline. Small detail, large distinction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How do presigned download headers fix the wrong filename and content type?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the generated file, not with the URL. If an export worker produces a PDF, set the media type from that fact. If it produces a CSV, set the CSV type. Do not infer a content type from an untrusted display name, and do not let a storage key such as &lt;code&gt;tenant-1842/job-771&lt;/code&gt; become the customer-facing name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then construct a content disposition that explicitly requests an attachment. A practical filename has two parts: an ASCII fallback for older or limited clients, and an encoded UTF-8 value for names that contain spaces or non-ASCII characters. The fallback should be a basename, never a user-supplied path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the policy layer I would put in front of any storage adapter. It does not know which object-storage implementation signs URLs; that separation is deliberate. The policy can be unit-tested without pretending that a signed link is a browser test.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;__future__&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;annotations&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unicodedata&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pathlib&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PurePath&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;urllib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quote&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="no"&gt;MEDIA_TYPES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;".csv"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"text/csv"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;".pdf"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/pdf"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ascii_fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unicodedata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"NFKD"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;plain&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ascii"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ignore"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;decode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ascii"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;plain&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"[^A-Za-z0-9._ -]"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"_"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;plain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;strip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;" ."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;plain&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"download"&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;export_headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PurePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValueError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"filename must be a non-empty basename"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PurePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lower&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;MEDIA_TYPES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValueError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"only PDF and CSV exports are supported"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ascii_fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'"'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"_"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;encoded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;quote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;safe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;MEDIA_TYPES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Disposition"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'attachment; filename="{fallback}"; '&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"filename*=UTF-8''{encoded}"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This fixes the policy inputs, not the persisted object by magic. The adapter must apply the returned values while uploading or through its supported metadata update operation, then read the object's metadata before asking for a fresh presigned link. A link issued before the correction belongs to the old delivery attempt; discard it and create another one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a second subtlety: the extension and declared type must agree with the bytes. Renaming a CSV to &lt;code&gt;.pdf&lt;/code&gt; makes the filename look right while leaving the client with the wrong expectation. A useful test fixture contains a real PDF, a real CSV, a name with spaces, a Unicode name, and a long name. I'm not sure which filename limit applies across every browser, proxy, and receiving filesystem, so I would test the actual delivery path instead of publishing a made-up universal limit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where does tenant isolation fail in the download path?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most dangerous design is one that checks the tenant once, then passes an object key around as if the key itself were proof. Keys are identifiers, not authorization statements. A download request should resolve an export record such as &lt;code&gt;(tenant_id, export_id, object_key, format, display_name)&lt;/code&gt; and compare the authenticated tenant before signing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A clean critical path looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The export job writes bytes under an opaque key owned by one tenant.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The application stores the format and intended display name beside the export record.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The download request checks the authenticated tenant against that record.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The service derives headers from the recorded format and sanitized basename.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The storage adapter verifies the object's metadata and creates a short-lived presigned link.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The browser test checks disposition, filename, media type, and bytes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not let a client submit a replacement &lt;code&gt;Content-Disposition&lt;/code&gt; and treat it as trusted. A client may request a label, but the server owns the final basename and must reject path fragments. Keep the object key opaque as well; an internal key can still disclose sequence numbers or account relationships through logs, referrers, or support screenshots.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The failure modes deserve separate alerts. A PDF displayed inline points at disposition. A downloaded file named after the object key points at filename handling. A CSV downloaded with a PDF association points at the declared type or the generated bytes. A cross-tenant result points at the authorization lookup, regardless of whether the signature itself was valid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One incident can contain more than one failure. That is why I would log a redacted export identifier, tenant identifier, selected format, metadata values, signing timestamp, expiry, and a result category. I would not log the full presigned URL: query parameters are credentials, even when they are short-lived.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which design trade-offs belong in the decision record?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are three reasonable places to enforce download behavior, and they do not provide the same guarantees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Choice&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Strength&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cost or boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Persist metadata on the object&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Every delivery starts from a durable, inspectable contract&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A metadata correction may require a supported rewrite or update, and a new link must be issued&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Add response overrides when signing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Useful when one object needs different presentation names&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The signing adapter must support the override, and stored metadata can still be wrong for other readers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Proxy the download through the application&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Centralizes tenant checks and response headers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application owns bandwidth, range requests, timeouts, and retry behavior&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For private media exports, I prefer durable metadata plus an application authorization check. A response override can be a valid exception for a one-off label, but it should not hide a bad upload contract. A proxy is a valid choice when policy inspection, audit requirements, or content transformation outweigh direct delivery efficiency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The catch is that this pattern is not suitable for every file-delivery product. If the requirement is a permanent public asset, static hosting, or anonymous sharing, a signed-only export path is the wrong abstraction; use a system whose access model explicitly supports that requirement. If the application needs object versioning, immutable retention, conditional writes, or sub-day automatic deletion, those controls must be verified separately rather than assumed from the presence of presigned links. The AWS lifecycle documentation is useful for checking expiry assumptions, and a worker platform may be appropriate for an application-controlled delivery layer, but neither source changes the tenant authorization invariant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should the fix be tested before deployment?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unit tests should cover header derivation and rejection of path-like names. Integration tests should prove that metadata survives the storage adapter and that a new signature is generated after a correction. The browser test is the one teams skip, usually because the API test is green.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would make the browser matrix small and intentional: one PDF and one CSV, each with a plain name, spaces, and Unicode. Assert the suggested filename, whether the browser downloads rather than opens inline, the response media type, and a hash or parser-level check of the bytes. Repeat after link expiry so the client exercises its authorized refresh flow instead of silently falling back to a public URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deployment should also preserve the ordering. Rolling out a new filename policy before the worker and adapter agree on format metadata creates mixed exports, while signing before the authorization record is committed creates a race between visibility and access. A feature flag can limit the new path to a test tenant, but the invariant itself should remain the same.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision is therefore straightforward: keep tenant authorization in the application, keep bytes and metadata aligned at write time, make attachment disposition explicit, and verify the actual browser behavior. Fixing a filename in frontend code is cosmetic if the response still says inline, and making an object public is an isolation regression disguised as a download fix.&lt;/p&gt;

